## 2026-10-30T12:12Z managed-agent OAuth flow (CreateVaultOAuthFlow)
- RedirectUrl validation is enforced server-side:
  - https://example.com/ -> `redirect_url origin "https://example.com" is not in allowlist`
  - https://console.byteplus.com.evil.example/ -> rejected (origin not allowlisted)
  - https://console.byteplus.com@example.com/ -> rejected (origin resolved to example.com)
  - https://console.byteplus.com%2eexample.com/ -> `not a valid absolute URL`
  - javascript:alert(1) and //example.com/ -> `not a valid absolute URL`
  - fragment URLs -> `redirect_url must not contain fragment`
  Conclusion: no open redirect / javascript: URL bypass in RedirectUrl.
- McpServerUrl is not an arbitrary SSRF primitive. With valid RedirectUrl and McpServerUrl pointing at tester-controlled callbacks (webhook.site token, requestcatcher subdomain), the API returned `UpstreamUnavailable: oauth metadata discovery failed ... metadata not found` and the callbacks received no requests. Only built-in MCP providers (Linear/Notion/GitHub etc. listed in the client bundle) are supported; unknown hosts are rejected before any successful metadata fetch.
- Vault OAuth callback route (`/managed-agents/credentials-vault-oauth/callback`) is static: reads vaultId/credential_id/source from the query string, writes a same-origin localStorage event, shows a success card, closes the window. No backend call and no attacker-controlled sink; low UI spoofing only (already recorded).
- Status: OAuth flow redirect/SSRF hypotheses falsified. Not Vulnerable.
